#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# q_binomial

#% text
# `q_binomial(n, k, q)` gives the q-binomial (Gaussian) coefficient `[n, k]_q`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `[n, k]_q` is `(q; q)_n/((q; q)_k*(q; q)_(n - k))` (DLMF 17.2.27); for integers it is a polynomial in `q` whose coefficients count the partitions in a `k` by `n - k` box.

#% item
# At `q = 1` it is `binomial(n, k)`.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
q_binomial(4, 2, q)
#% output
#= 1 + q + 2*q^2 + q^3 + q^4

#% cell
q_binomial(5, 2, 2)
#% output
#= 155

#% cell
q_binomial(6, 3, 0.5)
#% output
#= 2.724609375

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The number of 2-dimensional subspaces of the 4-dimensional vector space over the field with 3 elements:

#% cell
q_binomial(4, 2, 3)
#% output
#= 130

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# The limit `q -> 1`:

#% cell
q_binomial(6, 2, 1)
#% output
#= 15

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `q_pochhammer`, `q_factorial`, `binomial`
